{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Set `merge.ours.driver=true` in the reconciler workflow so the tickets branch's\n`.bridge_state/* merge=ours` attribute actually resolves on GHA. Latent bug today; hard prerequisite\nfor B1. session_log **1cb6-d553-6565-49b4** (measured facts; do not re-derive).\n\n## Why\n`init.py:508-511` sets `merge.ours.driver` **per clone**, and it is never committed;\n`reconcile-bridge.yml` never sets it. So on the runner the `merge=ours` attribute names an\n**undefined driver** and git silently falls back to a 3-way text merge of an **18,231,660-byte** JSON\nfile — the opposite of the local-wins behaviour the design assumes.\n\n**Scope correction (plan-review G6 remediation): the canary is NOT in scope.**\n`reconcile-bridge-canary.yml` does a bare `git worktree add` at `:88` but then calls **`rebar init`\nat `:146`**, and its own comment at `:138-140` explains why (\"a bare `git worktree add` of the\ntickets branch does NOT create ... and the write seam refuses every rebar write without it\"). So the\ncanary already gets `merge.ours.driver` via `_merge_ours_unit`. `reconcile-bridge.yml` does the bare\nworktree add at `:117` and **never** calls `rebar init`, which is why only it is affected. An earlier\ndraft listed both workflows; that was wrong.\n\nThis must land before B1: `blob:none` keeps commits but removes merge-base **blobs**, which the\nreconverge path materializes, so filtering while the driver is undefined makes an already-fragile\npath lazily fault large blobs mid-merge.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `reconcile-bridge.yml` sets `merge.ours.driver=true` for the tickets worktree before any merge\n      step. Tier: **unit** workflow-lint assertion.\n      Proving command: `pytest tests/unit/workflows/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py -v`.\n- [ ] The canary is asserted to obtain the driver via its existing `rebar init` call, so it needs no\n      edit — a regression guard against re-adding the change there. Tier: **unit** workflow-lint.\n- [ ] A reconverge against a **diverged** remote in a fresh runner-shaped checkout resolves\n      `.bridge_state/*` **local-wins** (not a text merge) and produces no conflict markers. Tier:\n      **integration** in a real temporary git repo mimicking the runner setup.\n- [ ] The same scenario **without** the config produces the text-merge behaviour — the negative\n      control proving the test detects the defect. Tier: **integration**.\n\n## Files\n`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`, tests (the canary is deliberately untouched)\n\nScope: XS-S. No dependencies."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785376970709719001,"uuid":"9fa4c3e3-fbb0-458a-9517-ada4b27826ca"}